;; components.edn — class macros + reusable components (codegen-expanded).
;;
;; Evicted from tokens.edn: tokens carry LAYER 1-2 design VALUES only;
;; class macros (@name expansions) and component definitions are
;; composition, not design values. Loaded as the sibling of tokens.edn
;; by the codegen entrypoints and merged for the pipeline.
{;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 ;; LAYER 3: CLASS MACROS — Reusable class string compositions.
 ;; Referenced via @name in class strings. No nesting allowed.
 ;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 :class-defs
 ;; NO LAYOUT-BEARING PROPERTY UNDER ANY STATE PREFIX IN ANY MACRO HERE.
 ;; lv_style.c flags a whole class of props LV_STYLE_PROP_FLAG_LAYOUT_UPDATE —
 ;; border width, width/height and their min/max, x/y, align, translate, every
 ;; pad and margin, text font and spacing, layout, base dir, and the flex/grid
 ;; families — and lv_obj_get_style_space_left adds the border width to the
 ;; padding on each side the border is drawn on. So a state style built from
 ;; any of them MOVES THE LAYOUT when that state is entered: it resizes a
 ;; w-content macro and shrinks the CONTENT box of a fixed-size one. The rule
 ;; is stated over the whole class rather than over `focused:border-w` alone,
 ;; because `pressed:p-*`, `focus-key:border-w-*` and `focused:font-*` are the
 ;; same defect and a narrower rule reads as permission for them.
 ;;
 ;; Dress a state with COLOUR, OPACITY or an OUTLINE instead.
 ;; LV_STYLE_BORDER_COLOR carries no layout flag, and the outline props carry
 ;; EXT_DRAW_UPDATE rather than LAYOUT_UPDATE — an outline is drawn OUTSIDE the
 ;; box, so it changes what is painted without moving anything.
 ;;
 ;; WHICH OF THE TWO A MACRO CAN USE DEPENDS ON WHETHER IT HAS A BASE BORDER.
 ;; @btn-secondary and @hud-btn carry one, so they recolour it. @btn-primary
 ;; carries none — a colour alone would paint at width 0 — so its ring is an
 ;; outline.
 ;;
 ;; AN OUTLINE NEEDS STANDOFF, and the PARENT INSET is only one of the two
 ;; things that can deny it. Its reach is outline_pad + outline_width — at the
 ;; base breakpoint @btn-primary's is 2 + 2 = 4px — and that reach is spent on
 ;; whatever already occupies those pixels, whichever is NEARER: the parent's
 ;; own inset, or the FLEX GAP to a sibling.
 ;;
 ;; A SIBLING IS REACHABLE AT ALL BECAUSE LAYOUT DOES NOT RESERVE FOR A RING.
 ;; That is the same EXT_DRAW_UPDATE-not-LAYOUT_UPDATE fact stated above, read
 ;; forwards: a flex parent measures and spaces by the DRAWN box, so it leaves
 ;; the ring exactly nothing and the ring lands on whoever is next. The UI
 ;; standard already states this shape for the other quantity that widens a
 ;; node invisibly — whoever sets `hit_slop` owes the space, because the slop
 ;; too "lands on whatever sits beside the node". An outline is the PAINT twin
 ;; of that REACH rule, and it is owed the same reservation.
 ;;
 ;; BOTH BOUNDS, WITH THE ARITHMETIC. @card gives
 ;; `lv_obj_get_style_space_left` = pad + border_width = 2 + 1 = 3px, so a
 ;; @btn-primary flush inside a @card overruns that card's box by 1px at base
 ;; (it fits from md: up, where spacing-sm widens the pad). On the sibling
 ;; side, a `gap-spacing-sm` row gives 4px, which the ring exactly fills — it
 ;; stops on the neighbour's first pixel and shares none, the same
 ;; touching-is-not-overlapping boundary the overlap lane draws at
 ;; :overlap/gap-px 0. A `gap-spacing-xs` row gives 2px, which is half of what
 ;; the ring needs. The 4px reach is calibrated to the former.
 ;;
 ;; THE GAP IS THE BOUND THAT BINDS IN PRACTICE, measured rather than assumed.
 ;; The one screen that puts this macro beside a sibling — kitchen_sink's
 ;; Buttons card — renders a 2px gap to @btn-secondary in EVERY theme family,
 ;; mode, canvas and breakpoint tier of the scratch matrix, while the inset
 ;; from its own parent measures 9px under asgard and 22-26px under
 ;; stock/vanilla. So the parent-inset advice is SATISFIED at every one of
 ;; those cells and the ring is defective at every one of them, which is why
 ;; naming the inset alone is worse than naming nothing: it sends an author to
 ;; check the bound that is already met.
 ;;
 ;; Its row is authored `gap-spacing-sm sm:gap-spacing-xs`, and that is the
 ;; trap to know before widening one — but the trap is SHARPER than "a
 ;; minimum tier rather than a band", and the sharper statement is what tells
 ;; an author what to do. `bp-min-index` in lvgl-codegen.style-props gives
 ;; `:sm` minimum composite index 0, and `expand->variants` applies a token at
 ;; index `idx` exactly when `idx >= min-idx` — so an `sm:` token applies at
 ;; all eight indices, which is the identical set a BARE token applies at.
 ;; `sm:X` and `X` are therefore indistinguishable at every canvas, and the
 ;; prefix can never SCOPE anything: its only reachable effect is to shadow
 ;; the base token beside it while reading as responsive. The base 4px never
 ;; applies anywhere, and the 2px is that shadowing — measured at 1920x1080
 ;; too.
 ;;
 ;; SO THE 2px IS NOT A SMALL-CANVAS CHOICE, and that matters because the two
 ;; readings prescribe opposite repairs. Every `sm:` token in the screen that
 ;; crowds this macro SHRINKS what it overrides — gap and pad from `-sm` to
 ;; `-xs`, and three widget boxes to smaller ones — while every other tier
 ;; prefix in this repository GROWS with the tier, this macro's own
 ;; `md:p-spacing-sm` included. Base-large with a smaller `sm:` beside it is
 ;; only meaningful under a MAX-width reading, which this DSL does not have,
 ;; so the row expresses an intent it cannot carry. The spelling that carries
 ;; it is the inverse — a base `-xs` widened by `md:` — which is the idiom the
 ;; macros here already use, and which still renders 2px at the base tier. A
 ;; reader who retires the override to "restore the authored gap" therefore
 ;; picks the LARGE value at every canvas, which is the one value that row's
 ;; author demonstrably did not want.
 ;;
 ;; THE SYMPTOM DEPENDS ON DRAW ORDER RATHER THAN ON THE OVERRUN, which is the
 ;; part most likely to mislead whoever diagnoses it.
 ;; renderer/lvgl/src/core/lv_refr.c walks children ASCENDING, so a button that
 ;; PRECEDES its neighbour has the shared pixels repainted by that neighbour's
 ;; own fill and its ring is visibly CUT on that side alone, while a button
 ;; that is the LAST child has nothing drawn after it and instead OCCLUDES
 ;; whatever it reached into. Same overrun, opposite appearance.
 ;;
 ;; THE CUT HALF IS NOW OBSERVED RATHER THAN ONLY REASONED, and it is the
 ;; reason vr_focus_crowding measures the ring on its FREE flank. Driven with
 ;; that fixture's gap tightened to the 2px the real screen renders, the
 ;; sibling flank shows ZERO changed pixels on focus while the free flank
 ;; still shows the full 4px: the neighbour repaints every pixel the ring put
 ;; inside it, so a framebuffer read taken toward the sibling reports an
 ;; ABSENT ring for an overrunning one. Nothing this repo SHIPS renders it,
 ;; though — the corpus that drives focused states (the devcards state cards)
 ;; is authored in the proto node shape and carries no class macros, and the
 ;; one screen that crowds this macro is never rendered focused.
 ;;
 ;; So give a focusable primary button 4px of clearance on every side that can
 ;; be crowded — parent inset AND sibling gap — not the inset alone.
 ;;
 ;; SPENDING THE PAD TO FIT A TIGHTER GAP IS REFUTED, not merely declined, and
 ;; the refutation is arithmetic over this repo's own tokens. Taking
 ;; `outline-pad` to `spacing-none` and leaving the width would halve the
 ;; reach to 2 — and it would put the ring flush on this macro's OWN fill,
 ;; where `focused-edge` against `accent-bg` measures 1.49:1 dark and 1.55:1
 ;; light. The ring is legible today only because the pad shows the surface
 ;; BEHIND the button between fill and stroke: against `surface-1` that same
 ;; ring measures 10.27:1 dark and 4.03:1 light, and against `surface-2`
 ;; 9.08:1 and 3.44:1. The pad is the contrast mechanism, not slack in a
 ;; budget, so a zero-pad ring would trade a neighbour's 2px for its own
 ;; legibility on every card.
 ;;
 ;; RECOLOURING DOES NOT RESCUE THAT, and the reason is the ring's TWO
 ;; neighbours rather than one: without a pad its inner edge borders the fill
 ;; and its outer edge the surface, so it owes a contrast floor to both at
 ;; once. Swept over every colour token this repo declares, SIX clear 3:1
 ;; against `accent-bg` in both modes — and all six are surface or ink
 ;; tokens that then fail against the surface side. ZERO clear 3:1 against
 ;; `accent-bg` and all three surfaces together. The pad is what removes the
 ;; inner obligation, which is why it is the mechanism and not the slack.
 ;;
 ;; AND SHRINKING THIS MACRO WOULD NOT CLEAR SUCH A ROW ANYWAY, because it is
 ;; not the only ring drawn there. The theme attaches its own focus outline to
 ;; a button's MAIN part on LV_STATE_FOCUS_KEY (renderer/src/theme.c), reach
 ;; `outline_pad + outline_width`: asgard takes THEME_BORDER_W + THEME_OUTLINE_W
 ;; = 1 + 2 = 3px, and stock/vanilla takes `dpx(dpi, 3)` twice, so 6px at the
 ;; reference DPI and more above it. Those land on @btn-secondary and on a
 ;; plain unmacro'd button, neither of which carries a class outline to
 ;; shrink, so a keypad focus in a 2px row still overruns after any change
 ;; confined here — and stock's reach does not fit a 4px gap either. The gap
 ;; is the bound that has to move; this ring is not where the fix is.
 ;;
 ;; Guarded by renderer/wasm_harness/tests/visual_regression.rs, mod
 ;; focus_geometry — vr_focus_geometry for the border case, vr_focus_outline
 ;; for the outline drawing at all, and vr_focus_crowding for THIS bound: a
 ;; row that gives the ring exactly the 4px prescribed above, asserting the
 ;; drawn ring reaches no further than the gap allows and is not cut on the
 ;; sibling side. The first two render a LONE button and guard no gap.
 {:body "font-font-body text-fg-0"
  ;; The focus ring is an OUTLINE, which is drawn outside the box and carries no
  ;; layout flag, so it is visible without moving anything. A border here would
  ;; not be: this macro is `w-content`, so a focus border would grow the widget
  ;; and reflow its row, which is the defect the rule above states.
  :btn-primary
  "w-content h-content p-spacing-xs bg-accent-bg rounded-radius-button
                pressed:bg-pressed-accent
                focused:outline-color-focused-edge
                focused:outline-w-border-width-focus
                focused:outline-pad-spacing-xs
                md:p-spacing-sm"
  :btn-secondary
  "w-content h-content p-spacing-xs bg-surface-2 rounded-radius-button
                  border-edge-0 border-w-border-width-default pressed:bg-pressed-surface
                  focused:border-color-focused-edge
                  md:p-spacing-sm"
  :caption "font-font-caption text-fg-1"
  :card
  "flex flex-col bg-surface-1 rounded-radius-card p-spacing-xs
                border-edge-0 border-w-border-width-default md:p-spacing-sm"
  :card-row
  "flex flex-row bg-surface-1 rounded-radius-card p-spacing-xs
                border-edge-0 border-w-border-width-default md:p-spacing-sm"
  :data-value "text-fg-0 font-font-data"
  :heading "font-font-heading text-fg-0"
  ;; The md:/xl: `border-w` tokens below are BREAKPOINT widths, not focus
  ;; styling — vr_hud_btn/vr_hud_col exist to verify exactly that ladder. A
  ;; sweep matching the `border-width-focus` TOKEN NAME guts them; match the
  ;; `focused:` PREFIX.
  :hud-btn
  "w-52 h-52 p-spacing-xs
                border-hud-border border-w-border-width-default
                bg-status-error opa-overlay-opa
                rounded-radius-button
                pressed:bg-pressed-accent pressed:opa-hud-pressed-opa
                focused:border-color-focused-edge
                disabled:opa-disabled-opa
                md:w-72 md:h-72 md:border-w-border-width-focus md:bg-status-warning
                lg:w-96 lg:h-96 lg:bg-status-success
                xl:w-120 xl:h-120 xl:border-w-border-width-bold xl:p-spacing-md xl:bg-accent-bg"
  :hud-label "text-accent-bg font-font-heading"
  :muted "font-font-caption text-fg-2"
  :tactical-btn
  "bg-accent-bg text-accent-text rounded-radius-button p-spacing-sm
                 font-font-body"
  :tactical-panel
  "bg-surface-1 border-edge-0 border-w-border-width-default
                   rounded-radius-card p-spacing-md"}
 ;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 ;; LAYER 4: COMPONENTS — Reusable widget templates with typed params.
 ;; Resolved at codegen time — renderer always gets flat LVGL widgets.
 ;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 :components
 {;; Labeled action button — the button idiom every screen otherwise spells as
  ;; an :lv_button wrapping one :lv_label child. :class is the BUTTON's full
  ;; class string (usually opening with a @btn-* macro); :label-class styles
  ;; the label and defaults to none, matching a bare authored label; :event is
  ;; optional because a gallery button demonstrates appearance and fires
  ;; nothing. Expands to exactly the tree the longhand spells — a usage is a
  ;; statement of intent ("a button labelled X firing Y"), not a new widget.
  :btn
  {:params {:class {:required true :type :string}
            :event {:type :keyword}
            :label {:required true :type :string}
            :label-class {:default "" :type :string}}
   :tree {:children [{:class "$label-class" :tag :lv_label :text "$label"}]
          :class "$class"
          :event :$event
          :tag :lv_button}}
  ;; Caption + live-value pair — one readout: a muted caption naming the
  ;; reading and a subject-bound value label beside it. The wrapper is :bare
  ;; (grouping without chrome) and the intra-pair gap is the word-space
  ;; :spacing-sm; the value carries @caption so its face matches the caption's
  ;; (equal face is the only route to equal baseline — LVGL flex has no
  ;; baseline cross-place) while its ink sits one contrast step above.
  :readout
  {:params {:label {:required true :type :string}
            :placeholder {:default "--" :type :string}
            :subject {:required true :type :keyword}}
   :tree {:bare true
          :children [{:class "font-font-caption text-fg-2" :tag :lv_label :text "$label"}
                     {:bind {:text :$subject}
                      :class "@caption"
                      :tag :lv_label
                      :text "$placeholder"}]
          :class "flex flex-row gap-spacing-sm w-content h-content"
          :tag :lv_obj}}
  :titled-card
  {:params {:extra-class {:default "" :type :string} :title {:required true :type :string}}
   :tree
   {:children [{:class "@caption" :tag :lv_label :text "$title"} {:slot :default}]
    :class
    "@card gap-spacing-xs w-content h-content $extra-class
                  md:gap-spacing-sm"
    :tag :lv_obj}}
  :status-led {:params {:brightness {:default 255 :type :int}
                        :color {:required true :type :map}
                        :label {:required true :type :string}}
               :tree {:children [{:class "w-36 h-36"
                                  :led_props {:brightness :$brightness :color :$color}
                                  :tag :lv_led}
                                 {:class "@muted" :tag :lv_label :text "$label"}]
                      :class "flex flex-col gap-spacing-xs h-content w-content"
                      :tag :lv_obj}}
  ;; Radio group — LVGL has no radio widget; the upstream idiom is
  ;; circle-styled checkboxes with one-active logic. The SUBJECT is the
  ;; group state: each option sets it via :event and mirrors it via
  ;; :checked-when; :flags-clear #{:checkable} kills the widget's
  ;; self-toggle so checked state is PURELY subject-driven (clicking the
  ;; active option cannot un-check it into an inconsistent state).
  :radio-group {:params {}
                :tree {:children [{:slot :default}]
                       :class "flex flex-col gap-spacing-xs w-content h-content"
                       :tag :lv_obj}}
  :radio-option {:params {:event {:required true :type :keyword}
                          :label {:required true :type :string}
                          :subject {:required true :type :keyword}
                          :value {:required true :type :int}}
                 :tree {:checked-when {:subject :$subject :value :$value}
                        :event :$event
                        :flags-clear #{:checkable}
                        :style {:indicator {:radius :radius-circle}}
                        :tag :lv_checkbox
                        :text "$label"}}
  ;; lv_demo_widgets shop-tab list row (create_shop_item in
  ;; lv_demo_widgets_shop.c): a bare grid cont — image spanning both
  ;; rows, name / muted category stacked, price right-aligned. Only the
  ;; price varies across the five instances.
  :demo-shop-item
  {:params {:price {:required true :type :string}}
   :tree {:bare true
          :children [{:cell {:col 0 :row 0 :row-span 2 :x-align :start :y-align :start}
                      :image_props {:src "P:images/demo/clothes.png"}
                      :tag :lv_image}
                     {:cell {:col 2 :row 0 :x-align :start :y-align :end}
                      :tag :lv_label
                      :text "Blue T-shirt"}
                     {:cell {:col 2 :row 1 :x-align :start :y-align :start}
                      :style {:text-opa 127}
                      :tag :lv_label
                      :text "Clothes"}
                     {:cell {:col 3 :row 0 :x-align :end :y-align :end}
                      :tag :lv_label
                      :text "$price"}]
          :class "w-pct-100 h-content"
          :grid-cols [:content 5 [:fr 1] [:fr 1]]
          :grid-rows [[:fr 1] [:fr 1]]
          :tag :lv_obj}}}}